Setup. Consider an invertible linear system $Ax=b$ with $A\in\mathbb{C}^{n\times n}$ a (possibly) nonnormal matrix, i.e., $A^*A\neq AA^*$. Let $S$ denote a relaxation (smoothing) iteration with error-propagation operator $E=I-M^{-1}A$ for some splitting $A=M-N$. An error vector $e\in\mathbb{C}^n$ is called algebraically smooth (with respect to $S$) when relaxation reduces it slowly, i.e., $\|Ee\|\approx\|e\|$. In a Galerkin-type two-grid algebraic multigrid scheme one chooses a restriction operator $R:\mathbb{C}^n\to\mathbb{C}^{n_c}$ and an interpolation operator $P:\mathbb{C}^{n_c}\to\mathbb{C}^n$, and the coarse-grid correction projects errors onto $\mathrm{range}(P)$ in a manner determined by $R$. For the method to converge rapidly, the coarse space $\mathrm{range}(P)$ must accurately approximate the set of algebraically smooth errors, and analogously $\mathrm{range}(R^*)$ must approximate algebraically smooth errors of the adjoint problem $A^*y=c$.

When $A$ is Hermitian (or more generally normal), the algebraically smooth errors are well-characterized as the span of eigenvectors of $A$ associated with small (in modulus) eigenvalues, equivalently the near-kernel of $A$, and this characterization guides the construction of $P$ (and $R$). When $A$ is nonnormal, however, singular vectors of $A$ associated with small singular values do not in general coincide with eigenvectors associated with small-modulus eigenvalues, and neither set need coincide with the algebraically smooth errors of a given relaxation $S$.

Problem. Provide a useful and computationally accessible characterization of the subspace of algebraically smooth errors that the interpolation operator $P$ (respectively the restriction $R$) must approximate on coarse levels of an algebraic multigrid hierarchy when $A$ is a general nonnormal matrix. In particular, identify a subspace $\mathcal{S}(A,S)\subseteq\mathbb{C}^n$ (and a dual subspace $\mathcal{S}^*(A,S)$ for $R$) such that:
(i) $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ contains all errors that are slow to be reduced by the smoother $S$ in a meaningful norm;
(ii) approximation of $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ by $\mathrm{range}(P)$ and of $\mathcal{S}^*(A,S)$ by $\mathrm{range}(R^*)$ implies a quantitative two-grid convergence bound bounded away from 1 independently of $n$;
(iii) $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ reduces, when $A$ is normal, to the standard near-kernel/small-eigenvalue characterization;
(iv) $\mathcal{S}(A,S)$ admits practical numerical access (e.g., via test vectors generated by relaxation, suitable Krylov processes, or singular/eigen computations of an associated normal operator).

